//? Title: Convolutional Networks: Shared Filters, Position and Pixel Relevance
//? Subtitle: Explicit tensor training on noisy bars and original MNIST images
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Classify an image while learning local features that can be reused across its
spatial positions. Shared convolution weights reduce parameter count, but a
prediction can still depend on where the resulting features appear. We derive
the shared computation and gradient, fit a small tensor CNN on noisy bars, and
compare local pooling, global pooling and a dense baseline on shifted positions.
Learned filters, actual confusion matrices and signed relevance redistributions
expose both successes and limits. An initialized-weight relevance control checks
the temptation to equate a bright heatmap with a faithful explanation, and a
separate original-MNIST example trains and evaluates the same kind of network.
Every fit is an explicit tensor optimizer loop in the complete C++ programs.
)

\toc

> One shared kernel and its summed gradient

Consider a single-channel 3×3 image and a 2×2 diagonal kernel. The same two
unit weights are applied to four overlapping image patches, giving the raw
cross-correlation map [[2,5],[0,2]]. It is cross-correlation because the kernel
is not flipped. For the sum of these four raw outputs, each kernel derivative
sums all input pixels occupying that offset across the four patches. The
result [[4,6],[3,5]] is measured below by tensor autodiff.

This derivative is for the sum of raw correlations. ReLU and max-pooling have
their own derivative rules: ReLU blocks nonpositive preactivations under this
library's convention, and max-pooling routes to the first maximum in each
window. The example's pooled value is five, but its pooled derivative would
therefore follow one winning location rather than all four locations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cnn_shared_kernel.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n=dm::nn;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto image=n::tensor({1,2,0,0,1,3,2,0,1},{1,3,3,1},n::DType::Float64);
  auto kernel=n::tensor({1,0,0,1},{2,2,1,1},n::DType::Float64);kernel.requires_grad_();
  n::Conv2DOptions options;options.padding=n::Padding::Valid;
  const auto raw=n::conv2d(image,kernel,options);
  const auto pooled=n::max_pool2d(n::relu(raw),2,2);
  const auto derivative=n::grad(n::sum(raw),{kernel})[0];
  std::print("| actual tensor quantity | row-major values |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| raw shared correlations | {} |\n| derivative of raw-output sum with respect to kernel | {} |\n| ReLU then max-pool | {} |\n",raw.to_vector<double>(),derivative.to_vector<double>(),pooled.to_vector<double>());
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(60,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=4.6;node.height=2.9;node.font_size=20;
  diagram.node("input",{0,0},"Input [1,3,3,1]\n1  2  0\n0  1  3\n2  0  1",node)
         .node("kernel",{6.2,0},"One shared 2x2 kernel\n1  0\n0  1\nVALID, stride one",node);
  const auto values=raw.to_vector<double>();node.fill={220,252,231};
  diagram.node("map",{12.4,0},std::format("Raw map [1,2,2,1]\n{:.0f}  {:.0f}\n{:.0f}  {:.0f}\nsum {:.0f}",values[0],values[1],values[2],values[3],n::sum(raw).item()),node)
         .edge("input","kernel","patches").edge("kernel","map","slide")
         .text({6.2,3},"Shared weights sum contributions from every application",23)
         .text({6.2,-3},std::format("Actual d(sum raw)/d(kernel) = {}",derivative.to_vector<double>()),20)
         .save_svg("build/cnn_shared_kernel.svg");
  const auto before=n::tensor({0,1,0,0},{1,1,4,1}),shifted=n::tensor({0,0,1,0},{1,1,4,1});
  std::print("\nA one-cell shift across a stride-two boundary changes local maxima: {} -> {}.\n",n::max_pool2d(before,{1,2},{1,2}).to_vector<float>(),n::max_pool2d(shifted,{1,2},{1,2}).to_vector<float>());
}
```
