This deliberately easy, low-noise task reaches perfect accuracy on this held-out
split. It is a result for one generated dataset, initialization and training
schedule, not a natural-image benchmark or proof of positional generalization.
The next experiments keep those distinctions visible.

> The learned filters, on one shared signed scale

A convolution kernel here has shape [3,3,1,8]. Reordering its axes gives eight
3×3 grayscale tiles. All tiles below use the same symmetric range [−M,+M],
where M is the largest absolute learned coefficient; black is negative,
mid-grey is zero and white is positive. Each filter is not independently
rescaled. This makes differences in magnitude visible as well as pattern.

The figure displays actual learned values after the explicit fit. Local
contrasts can be inspected, but an attractive pattern alone does not establish
that a filter is a particular human-labelled edge detector or that it caused
the model's predictions. The initial-to-final kernel difference confirms that
these shared weights were updated, rather than supplied as fixed features.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cnn_filters.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n=dm::nn;namespace m=n::models;n::set_num_threads(2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");n::NoGradGuard guard;
  n::ParameterStore trained(1),initial(1);const auto probe=n::zeros({1,12,12,1});
  (void)m::SmallImageCnn(trained,probe);(void)m::SmallImageCnn(initial,probe);
  trained.load("build/cnn_bars.npz");initial.load("build/cnn_initial.npz");
  const auto weights=trained.at("cnn/conv/W");const auto maximum=n::max(n::abs(weights)).item();
  const auto change=n::sqrt(n::sum(n::square(weights-initial.at("cnn/conv/W")))).item();
  const auto tiles=n::transpose(weights,{3,0,1,2});std::vector<std::string> labels;
  for(int f=0;f<8;++f)labels.push_back(std::format("filter {}",f));
  auto picture=n::diagram_tensor_images(tiles,4,labels,-maximum,maximum,24);
  picture.text({9,4},"Learned kernels: one shared signed colour scale",19)
         .save_svg("build/cnn_filters.svg");
  std::print("| learned kernel shape | common absolute colour limit M | initial-to-final kernel L2 difference |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n",weights.shape(),maximum,change);
  if(!(change>0))return 1;
}
