// result_end
\caption(Top row: actual held-out inputs. Middle: trained signed relevance. Bottom: initialized weights at the same targets. Both map rows share one symmetric raw-score scale; grey is zero.)
\alttext(Four original bar inputs sit above trained and initialized same-target relevance maps. White and black indicate positive and negative signed relevance relative to a grey zero.)

\image(figures/cnn_relevance_contrast.svg)
\caption(The same horizontal input is explained for each class. A negative raw class score can produce a negative map rather than an absence of redistributed evidence.)
\alttext(One horizontal input is beside its horizontal-target and vertical-target signed maps at the same symmetric scale.)

A high on-bar fraction in both trained and initialized models limits what this
check establishes. It shows where this rule allocates absolute score mass; it
does not by itself show that learning discovered the causal object, that a
particular pixel intervention would change the decision, or that the relevance
map faithfully summarizes all negative and bias contributions. Shared raw-score
scales also make the initialized maps visually weaker when their seed logits
are smaller; independently normalizing every map would conceal that difference.

> Training early positions, testing late positions

Now fitting bars start only at rows or columns 0..3, while held-out bars start
at 8..10. These are full-width horizontal or full-height vertical bars, not
small objects moving diagonally from a top-left corner. Each split has 250
examples per class, with independent generation seeds seven and eight. The
three models share the same observations. Both CNNs use sixteen hidden units;
the dense baseline uses thirty-two. All use explicit SGD at 0.05 and batch
size sixteen. CNNs run forty epochs and the dense baseline runs two hundred,
preserving the original comparison's unequal optimization budgets.

This tests this particular positional distribution shift, not universal
translation invariance. Finite valid-convolution boundaries and independent
noise can change the set of feature responses even with global pooling.
Global max exactly forgets spatial arrangement of a given feature map; it
does not repair all boundary or representation changes in an input image.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cnn_shift_global_confusion.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n=dm::nn;namespace m=n::models;n::set_num_threads(2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::filesystem::create_directories("figures");
  n::BarImageOptions positions;positions.min_position=0;positions.max_position=3;
  const auto fit=n::make_bar_images(250,7,positions);
  positions.min_position=8;positions.max_position=10;const auto test=n::make_bar_images(250,8,positions);
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"local CNN","global CNN","dense baseline"},files{"cnn_shift_local_confusion.svg","cnn_shift_global_confusion.svg","cnn_shift_dense_confusion.svg"};
  std::print("| model | learned scalars | epochs | actual optimizer calls | fit accuracy | shifted accuracy | shifted NLL |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const int model:{0,2,1}) {
    m::SmallImageCnnConfig config;config.hidden=16;config.global_pool=model==1;
    n::ParameterStore p(1);
    auto forward=[&](const n::Tensor& image) {
      return model==2?m::DenseImageBaseline(p,image,32,2):m::SmallImageCnn(p,image,config).logits;
    };
    { n::NoGradGuard guard;(void)forward(n::take_rows(fit.images,{0})); }
    n::OptimizerConfig rule;rule.kind=n::OptimizerKind::Sgd;
    n::Optimizer optimizer(p,.05,rule);n::BatchOrder batches(500,16,1);
    const int epochs=model==2?200:40;std::size_t updates=0;
    for(int e=0;e<epochs;++e) {
      batches.begin_epoch();
      for(auto rows=batches.next();!rows.empty();rows=batches.next()) {
        const auto loss=n::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(forward(n::take_rows(fit.images,rows)),n::take_rows(fit.labels,rows));
        optimizer.minimize(loss);++updates;
      }
    }
    n::NoGradGuard guard;const auto scores=forward(test.images);
    n::ConfusionMatrix confusion(2);confusion.add(test.labels,n::argmax(scores,1));
    n::ConfusionMatrixPlotOptions options;options.labels={"horizontal","vertical"};options.title=names[model]+": actual shifted-position predictions";
    confusion.save_svg("build/"+files[model],options);
    if(model!=1)std::filesystem::copy_file("build/"+files[model],"figures/"+files[model],std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.9f} |\n",names[model],p.count(),epochs,updates,n::accuracy(forward(fit.images),fit.labels),confusion.accuracy(),n::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(scores,test.labels).item());
  }
}
