> Checks, interpretation and limits

The native implementation separates reusable data generation and model forwards
from every visible fitting loop. Its 21 new cases pass address and undefined-
behaviour sanitizers, alongside eight legacy CNN regressions and five directory
cases. Hand-computed forward values and shared-kernel derivatives, central
finite differences for every learned value, an independent convolutional
redistribution oracle, original RNG-stream equality, pooling counterexamples,
parameter counts, actual architecture traces, square image cells, signed seeds,
zero positive paths, input/range checks and preserved gradient state are tested.
All 62 affected definitions, fourteen compiled documentation examples and four
representative hovers pass. These are numerical and API checks, not a proof of
universal generalization or explanatory faithfulness.

The random-position bars are learned successfully on this split. Shifted
positions expose the location dependence of flattening; global max helps here,
with a finite-boundary/noise limit that its exact feature-map permutation
identity cannot remove. Learned filters really changed, but their appearance
alone does not identify their semantic role. The initialized-weight relevance
control and signed class contrast limit the earlier claim that on-object
heatmaps establish the model's causal reasons. Finally, a small original-MNIST
fit shows the same tensor workflow on real digit images while retaining the
source, split, checkpoint-selection and sample-size qualifications.

> References

- Y. LeCun, L. Bottou, Y. Bengio and P. Haffner (1998), *Gradient-based learning
  applied to document recognition*, Proceedings of the IEEE 86(11), 2278–2324,
  https://doi.org/10.1109/5.726791. The networks here are custom small ReLU
  classifiers, not LeNet-5 or pretrained paper models.
- S. Bach et al. (2015), *On Pixel-Wise Explanations for Non-Linear Classifier
  Decisions by Layer-Wise Relevance Propagation*, PLOS ONE 10(7), e0130140,
  https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0130140.
- G. Montavon et al. (2017), *Explaining nonlinear classification decisions with
  deep Taylor decomposition*, Pattern Recognition 65, 211–222,
  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patcog.2016.11.008. The report uses a stabilized
  signed-logit adaptation with explicit limits, not an exact conservation claim.
- Official torchvision MNIST resource names, mirror and digest definitions:
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pytorch/vision/main/torchvision/datasets/mnist.py.
  These are source-provenance metadata; every program here runs native C++.
- Canonical helpers: include/datamunge/nn/bar_images.hpp,
  include/datamunge/nn/small_image_models.hpp,
  include/datamunge/nn/zplus_relevance.hpp and
  include/datamunge/nn/vision_plots.hpp, with implementations in src/datamunge/nn/.
